{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And this for Judah.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): 'And this is the blessing that belongs to Judah,' that is, what he said: 'Hear, O HaShem, the voice of Judah' when he comes to war. At first. 'His hands are for him' (ידיו רב לו) — that his hands suffice him and he needs no help from a fleshly arm. Or one may say: 'his hands are for him' — his hands contend against him. And Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote: Judah was placed next to Reuben because both of them acknowledged (הודו) the wrongdoing that was in their hands. And they expounded in tractate Makkot, chapter 'These are the exiles': all forty years that Israel walked in the wilderness, the bones of Judah were rolling in the ark, because of the ban he had accepted upon himself, as it says (Genesis 44:32): ״וחטאתי לאבי כל הימים״ — 'and I shall have sinned against my father all the days.' Said Moses: What caused Reuben to acknowledge (שיודה)? Judah acknowledged (יהודה) — 'וזאת ליהודה.' Immediately: 'Hear, O HaShem, the voice of Judah.' Immediately his limbs entered the grave. And he had not yet ascended to the academy of the firmament — 'ואל עמו תביאנו.' And he did not yet know how to raise and state difficulties in the Mishnah — 'ידיו רב לו.' And he did not yet know how to resolve objections according to the halakhah — 'ועזר מצריו תהיה.' And his bones were still rolling in the ark, for he did not have the strength to ascend to the academy on high, since he had accepted upon himself the ban in this world and in the world to come, as it says (ibid.): 'all the days.' And what they said, 'his limbs entered the grave,' means his bones joined bone to bone and flesh crept over him from above. And he still did not have the strength to ascend, so he prayed for him: 'ואל עמו תביאנו.' And what they said, 'What caused Reuben to acknowledge — Judah acknowledged,' even though Judah's deed came later: there was a tradition in the hands of Moses our master, peace be upon him, transmitted from person to person, that Reuben did not acknowledge that he had disturbed his father's bed until the end of many years, after Judah had acknowledged his sin with Tamar; and at that time Reuben acknowledged that he had disturbed his father's bed — then all his brothers emerged from suspicion." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'וזאת ליהודה' — this is the attribute of Malkhut, from which the kingship belongs to the seed of Judah, and it is what grants him the power to prevail in his wars. And so David, who is of his seed, said (Psalms 27:3): ״אם תקום עלי מלחמה בזאת אני בוטח״ — 'though war arise against me, in this I trust,' for it is the attribute of David. And so it says (Psalms 99) ״ועוז מלך משפט אהב״, and it is written (Psalms 72) ״משפטיך למלך תן״. And therefore David prevailed in all the wars and defeated all his enemies, and therefore he always mentioned in his songs: 'למנצח מזמור לדוד' — 'For the victor, a psalm of David' — giving thanks to the Holy One, blessed be He, for He is the true victor, for insofar as he prevails, his prevailing is only from the side of that attribute. And because David held fast to that attribute and spilled much blood, he was not deemed fit to build the House of Mercy until Solomon his son came — a man of peace, who held fast to the attribute of justice (צדקה), as it says (ibid.): ״וצדקתך לבן מלך״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:02.850866421Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}